The project aimed to increase chickpea productivity and production through innovation platform approach in Ethiopia. To achieve the goal, the project’s specific objectives were:
1. To identify and prioritize constraints of chickpea production in Ethiopia;
2. To quantify the economic losses due to the identified constraints;
3. To evaluate the performance of promising chickpea varieties and cropping systems under different agro-ecological conditions;
4. To identify and evaluate chickpea cultivars and cropping systems for their potential to contribute to sustainable intensification in Ethiopia;
5. To assess risk attitudes and capacities of chickpea value chain actors;
6. To develop guidelines for site-specific innovation platforms to enhance chickpea production and productivity in Ethiopia.
To achieve the project objectives, it has undertaken the following major activities:
1. Constraints identification and prioritization of chickpea production in Ethiopia;
2. Determination of economic losses due to the identified constraints;
3. Evaluation of promising chickpea cultivars and cropping systems under different agro-ecological conditions;
4. Identification and evaluation of chickpea cultivars and cropping systems for their potential to contribute to sustainable intensification in Ethiopia;
5. Assessment of risk attitudes and capacities of chickpea value chain actors;
6. Development of guidelines for site-specific innovation platforms to enhance chickpea production and productivity in Ethiopia.
The project had the following major accomplishments:
1. Identification and prioritization of major constraints of chickpea production in Ethiopia. Major constraints of chickpea production in Ethiopia are poor seed multiplication system, lack of reliable and well balanced fertilization, lack of appropriate and sufficient crop water, inadequate and late crop protection, poor soil health, inadequate and insufficient crop nutrition, inadequate crop establishment, insufficient and late crop fertilization, and major insect-pests and diseases. Among these constraints, lack of reliable and well balanced fertilization, and inadequate and insufficient crop nutrition were prioritized for further research and technology development;
2. Determination of economic losses due to the identified constraints: Economic losses due to the identified constraints are US$ 556.87 million per year;
3. Evaluation of 20 promising chickpea cultivars and their performance under different cropping systems in four sites in Ethiopia. The result of the evaluation showed promising cultivars with high yield potential under different agro-ecological conditions;
